INT. MAYA'S BEDROOM - MORNING

The morning sun shines through the window.

Maya sits on the edge of her mattress. She is a nine-year-old wheelchair user with a spinal cord injury.

Maya pulls her manual wheelchair close. She transfers from the bed to the wheelchair seat in one smooth motion.

Maya stretches her arms. She puts on her fingerless gloves.

She picks up her totem from the nightstand. It is a small adjustable wrench on a bright orange lanyard. She loops it around her neck.

Maya rolls to her desk. She grabs a blueprint of a soapbox racer. She smiles.

MAYA
Today is the day. The Golden Sprocket is mine.

INT. MAYA'S GARAGE - DAY

The garage is full of tools. A half-built soapbox racer sits in the center. It is painted teal.

Maya rolls around the racer. She uses her wrench to tighten the steering column.

Her hands move fast. She spins the front wheel. It spins perfectly.

Instead of foot pedals, her racer has two hand-cranks connected to the rear axle.

Her friend, Sarah, walks into the garage. Sarah carries a box of orange decals.

SARAH
Are the hand-cranks ready, Maya?

MAYA
Ready and geared. I can spin these faster than anyone can pedal.

SARAH
Billy is down the street. He has a new metal chassis. He says he will leave us in the dust.

MAYA
Let him try. My Teal Tornado is built for speed.

EXT. OAK CREEK HILL - DAY

Dark clouds gather over the town of Oak Creek, Oregon.

The downhill race course is marked with orange cones.

Billy stands next to his massive gold-painted racer. He looks at Maya's racer.

Billy walks over. He rests his elbow on Maya's wheelchair push-handle.

Maya looks up. Her face is serious.

MAYA
Please do not lean on my chair, Billy.

Billy steps back, raising his hands.

BILLY
Sorry. Just looking. You really think those hand-wheels can beat my foot-pedals?

MAYA
I do not think. I know.

Suddenly, a loud crack of thunder rolls.

Rain pours down in sheets.

Within minutes, the street at the bottom of the hill turns into a rushing river.

Mr. Henderson, the race coordinator, blows his whistle.

MR. HENDERSON
The race is cancelled! The bottom of the hill is flooded!

The crowd of children groans.

BILLY
No way! I spent all month on this!

Maya looks at the water. She looks at the slope. She plans three moves ahead.

MAYA
Wait! We do not need to cancel.

MR. HENDERSON
Maya, the town square is under two feet of water. No soapbox car can drive through that.

MAYA
Our cars do not need to drive. They can skim.

EXT. OAK CREEK HILL - CONTINUOUS

Maya rolls her racer forward. She points to the wheels.

MAYA
Look. My wheels have hollow plastic hubs. They float. And the side panels are angled like a boat hull.

Sarah steps up.

SARAH
She is right! We can run a water-skimmer derby!

The other kids cheer.

Billy looks skeptical.

BILLY
My car is heavy steel. It will sink!

MAYA
Not if we strap these empty plastic jugs to your sides.

Maya pulls some empty water jugs from her toolkit. She tosses them to Billy.

BILLY
Why are you helping me?

MAYA
Because I want to beat you fairly, Billy.

Mr. Henderson looks at the course. He smiles.

MR. HENDERSON
Alright, racers! Line up!

EXT. STARTING LINE - DAY

The rain continues to fall.

Maya sits in her teal racer. She grips the hand-cranks.

Billy is next to her in his gold racer, now equipped with plastic jugs.

MR. HENDERSON
On your marks! Get set! Go!

Mr. Henderson drops the flag.

Maya cranks her arms. The Teal Tornado shoots forward.

The racers speed down the steep asphalt street.

Maya steers with precision. She avoids the slick patches of leaves.

Billy is right beside her. He pedals with all his might.

EXT. FLOODED CANAL - DAY

The street slopes down into the flooded town square.

The racers hit the water with a massive splash.

Billy's car slows down. His tires lose traction on the flooded road.

Maya's racer floats.

Maya continues to crank the hand-paddles. The angled fins on her wheels push her through the water.

She moves swiftly.

MAYA
Yes! It works!

Billy's car is drifting sideways. He is struggling to move.

BILLY
I cannot get any grip!

MAYA
Keep your steering straight! Use the current!

EXT. FLOODED TOWN SQUARE - DAY

The water is deeper here. The town square steps are submerged.

A concrete barrier sits just below the water's surface.

Billy's car hits the barrier with a thud. He is stuck.

BILLY
I am stuck on the steps!

Maya approaches the barrier. She knows this spot. She deals with these steps every day.

She looks at her totem wrench around her neck.

She pulls the wrench. She quickly adjusts the tension bolt on her front axle paddle.

She leans back in her seat. This shifts her center of gravity, lifting the front end of her racer.

She cranks hard.

The teal racer glides right over the submerged concrete step.

Billy watches in awe.

BILLY
How did you do that?

MAYA
I know how to clear a kerb!

She steers toward the finish line.

EXT. FINISH LINE - DAY

The finish line banner hangs between two lamp posts over the water.

Maya cranks her arms. The Teal Tornado glides across the water.

She crosses the finish line.

The crowd erupts in cheers.

Sarah runs into the shallow water, splashing.

SARAH
You did it! You won!

Maya raises her fists in victory. She is glowing with joy.

Billy floats across the finish line a moment later. He smiles.

BILLY
That was incredible, Maya. You are the fastest engineer in Oak Creek.

EXT. PODIUM - DAY

The rain has stopped. The sun breaks through the clouds.

A wooden winner's podium stands in the town square.

Mr. Henderson holds the Golden Sprocket Trophy.

Maya rolls up to the podium.

She locks her wheelchair brakes.

Maya transfers from her wheelchair onto the high wooden podium bench.

She sits proudly on the first-place spot.

Mr. Henderson hands her the trophy.

The crowd cheers.

Maya lifts the shiny gold trophy high.

She looks at her wheelchair waiting below the podium. It is her custom-built speed machine.

She transfers back down into her wheelchair, holding the trophy in her lap.

She spins a victory circle.